; ============================================================================================
; BT-9b SECTION PALETTE -- THE VALUE LADDER. (2026-08-28. Supersedes BT-9's hue-only palette.)
;
; WHY THIS EXISTS. BT-9 shipped a palette that passed every acceptance criterion on a sphere
; sheet and then failed in all four sections that consumed it. It separated in HUE and not in
; VALUE: ground and neutral sat 1.26-6.02 CIE L* apart, so on a desaturating night curve and
; against a bright day sky they collapsed into one surface. BT-4 measured cyan at 2.9% and had
; to drop `cyan_neutral` to ship; BT-5 reported hub ground and neutral both near-white; BT-2
; and BT-3 carried the same defect by construction. Hue is the first thing a night curve takes
; away. Value is what survives it, which is why value is now the FIRST axis and hue the second.
;
; THE LADDER -- three iso-lightness rungs, identical in every section:
;
;     RUNG 1  GROUND    L* = 83.9    pale, section-tinted (HSV S=0.08), hub untinted
;     RUNG 2  NEUTRAL   L* = 57.91   one mid grey, six paths -- `neutral_gray.tres` verbatim
;     RUNG 3  GOAL      L* = 37.0    saturated (HSV S=0.86), the one chromatic surface
;
;     Minimum adjacent-rung separation: 20.91 L*.  Before this pass: 1.26 L*.
;
; WHY IT SURVIVES THE NIGHT, WHICH IS THE POINT AND IS ARITHMETIC RATHER THAN TASTE. A night
; curve is a MULTIPLICATIVE scale on incident light, and a multiplicative scale preserves
; luminance RATIOS while desaturation destroys hue differences. The ladder is built on ratios:
; ground:neutral is now 2.47x in linear Y (it was 0.93x -- i.e. the ground was very slightly
; DARKER than the geometry standing on it), and neutral:goal is 2.72x. Those two numbers are
; invariant under `OutdoorAtmosphere`'s night ambient floor, whatever it is set to, which is
; also why this pass does NOT touch that floor and does not move THRILL-BIBLE 6.2's fork.
;
; SECTION 4 RULE 7 IS SATISFIED MORE LITERALLY THAN BEFORE, NOT LESS. "Ground = a very pale
; tint of the section colour" -- the tint is unchanged at HSV S=0.08, only its VALUE moved.
; "Most geometry neutral gray/white" -- it is now actually gray rather than a second white.
; "Only the goal gets the saturated colour" -- the goal is the only surface in the set above
; 0.3 chroma. Talon's "don't overdo it" constrains SATURATION and saturation is untouched; it
; never asked for geometry that cannot be seen.
;
; THE TWO NEUTRALS ARE NOW ONE COLOUR. BT-9 shipped both an off-white `<section>_neutral` and a
; mid `neutral_gray`; BT-4 abandoned the first for the second mid-packet. Rather than retire
; either PATH -- the layout contract's convention is `<section>_neutral.tres` and four sections
; reference it, while CyanRun references `neutral_gray` directly, and no section .tscn is this
; packet's to edit -- both paths now carry ONE colour. There is no second neutral to drift.
; ============================================================================================
;
; --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; DARK-1 / DARK-1b AMENDMENT (2026-08-29). THE LADDER MOVED DOWN INTACT; THE GROUND GOT BOLDER
; AND GOT A CHECKER.
;
; Talon, 2026-08-28: the level "looks clinical and sterile rather than atmospheric" and had
; "drifted into a bright white test lab look" -- then "I would like bolder overall" -- then, in
; the ruling of the same day, "the super bright light colours was too much ... darker in general"
; AND "see both light and dark". Measured over the shipped captures, 91% of every day frame sat
; at or above luminance 192 and 0.3% below 64: the frame had no dark end at all.
;
; ONE MULTIPLIER, APPLIED TO EVERY RUNG. Every albedo in this set was multiplied by 0.56 in
; LINEAR luminance. That number is not a taste pick -- it is what lands the rendered ground plate
; near screen luminance 110 instead of 141 under this world's graded daylight. Because it is one
; multiplier it preserves BT-9b's actual invariant, the linear-Y RATIO between rungs, EXACTLY:
;
;     RUNG 1  GROUND    L* 41.7   was 54.00 (DARK-1) / 83.90 (BT-9b)   section-tinted, HSV S=0.30
;     RUNG 2  NEUTRAL   L* 24.4   was 33.00 / 57.91                    one mid grey, six paths
;     RUNG 3  GOAL      L* 34.3   was 45.00 / 37.00                    HSV S=0.86, the beacon
;
;     ground:neutral = 2.91x in linear Y (BT-9b shipped 2.93x, DARK-1 2.92x). UNCHANGED.
;
; STATED PLAINLY BECAUSE IT IS THE ONE THING THAT GOT WORSE: the ground:neutral gap in L* is now
; 17.3, where DARK-1 measured 21.0 and BT-9b 20.91. That is arithmetic, not drift -- L* is a cube
; root, so ANY uniform darkening compresses a value ladder in L* while leaving its luminance
; ratios untouched, and "darker in general" cannot be delivered without paying it. Two things buy
; the legibility back rather than pretending the number did not move: the ground rung now carries
; a 1 m world-space CHECKER (bubbletest_ground_checker.gdshader), so ground-vs-neutral separates
; by PATTERN as well as by value; and ground saturation rises 0.18 -> 0.30.
;
; WHY SATURATION HAD TO RISE. Chroma is S x V. BT-9b's S=0.08 at V=0.838 gave absolute chroma
; 0.067; DARK-1 raised it to S=0.18 at V~0.53 for 0.095. Left there, this pass's V~0.41 would
; give 0.074 and the sections would start losing their names -- which is the one hard line in the
; brief ("testers must still be able to say the green section"). S=0.30 holds absolute chroma at
; ~0.124, above everything shipped so far. That IS the "bolder", and it is spent on the section
; hues themselves, never on a grade laid over the level.
;
; NOT A HUE MOVE, AND THAT IS A CONSTRAINT RATHER THAN A PREFERENCE. THRILL-BIBLE 10's "colour
; temperature as threat channel" is `spent-here` in this level on `lake_murk.tres`, and the
; ledger's own words are that a second graded surface "makes the first one decor". This pass
; darkens in VALUE and widens each section's OWN hue; it does not cool the level.
;
; WHAT DID THE REST OF THE WORK -- most of the darkening is NOT here. It is the per-world daylight
; profile in `BubbleTestWorld` (sun x0.62, ambient x0.34, sky and fog colour x0.42) plus the first
; real sun shadow in the repo. Albedo alone could not have done it: with nothing in the world able
; to occlude anything, no surface value produces a dark end to a histogram.
; --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

; GREEN GOAL -- HSV(130, 0.86, 0.368). Chroma 0.316.
; The HUE and the SATURATION are BT-9's, unchanged. Only the VALUE moved.
; WHY THE GOAL IS THE DARKEST RUNG AND NOT THE BRIGHTEST. A saturated colour cannot also be
; light -- there is no light saturated red or blue -- so a goal rung placed ABOVE the neutral is
; reachable for cyan and green and impossible for the other three, which would put the sections
; back on a per-hue rule. Below the neutral, one rule fits all five. The old set proved the
; point from the other side: cyan_goal sat at L* 77.09 and green_goal at 76.14, within 11 L* of
; their own grounds, while blue_goal sat at 38.88 -- a 'rung' 38 L* wide.
; Chroma still does the wayfinding: this surface is 43x the neutral's chroma and it is the only
; thing in the section above 0.3. Value now does the LEGIBILITY, which is the half that survives
; dusk.
; RUNG 3 of the ladder. Measured L* = 34.28. (DARK-1b, 2026-08-29; was 45.00 under DARK-1.)
[gd_resource type="StandardMaterial3D" format=3]

[resource]
albedo_color = Color(0.052, 0.368, 0.104, 1)
roughness = 0.62
metallic = 0.0
metallic_specular = 0.35
